<TopicPage slug="subjects/physiotherapy/foundational/the-movement-sciences">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Physiotherapy is applied movement science. Behind every manipulation, exercise programme and
    gait re-education sit three intersecting disciplines: <strong>biomechanics</strong> (the body
    as a system of levers and loads), <strong>exercise physiology</strong> (the body as an energy
    system that adapts to demand) and <strong>motor control and learning</strong> (the nervous
    system as the orchestrator that must actually produce the movement). A clinician who can read
    a movement problem in all three languages can tell whether a patient's difficulty is weak,
    stiff, unskilled — or a mix — and prescribe accordingly.
  </p>
  <p>
    A single case shows why three languages are needed. A 68-year-old cannot rise from a low
    chair without pushing off the armrests. The biomechanist notices the physics: rising requires
    the legs to generate large hip and knee torques from a deep-flexion position, where the
    muscles' leverage is poor, and the armrests are simply adding force at a long moment arm. The
    exercise physiologist asks whether the quadriceps can generate the needed force at all —
    and knows that eight weeks of progressive sit-to-stand training can add the missing torque.
    The motor-learning specialist asks whether the patient's strategy is the problem — many
    patients shift their weight too far back, loading the weakest position, and a cue ("nose over
    toes") changes the task more cheaply than any strength gain could. Weak, unfit or unskilled?
    Usually a little of each, in different proportions per patient — which is precisely why the
    three sciences are taught together, and why "physiotherapy" is not a synonym for massage or
    machines.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the working equations of the treatment room: torque, work, power and training heart rate" widget="equationStep" config={torqueCfg} />

  <h2>Kinesiology and biomechanics</h2>
  <p>
    Kinesiology describes movement — flexion, abduction, gait phases, range of motion;
    biomechanics explains it with forces. The skeleton is a lever system, and the lever classes
    matter clinically. A first-class lever (seesaw) has the fulcrum between force and load; a
    second-class lever (wheelbarrow) puts the load between; a <strong>third-class lever</strong>
    puts the effort between fulcrum and load — and that is the body's dominant design: the biceps
    inserts on the forearm a few centimetres from the elbow, while the load sits at the hand maybe
    30 cm away. The mechanical advantage (muscle moment arm divided by load moment arm) is
    therefore tiny, around 0.1, which means the muscle must pull with roughly <em>ten times</em>
    the weight held — a 5 kg dumbbell demands some 50 kg (about 490 N) of biceps force, and the
    elbow joint experiences a compressive reaction of similar magnitude. Why would evolution choose
    such a "bad" lever? Because the same geometry buys speed and range: the hand travels ten times
    further and faster than the muscle shortens. Strength and reach are a trade, and the human
    body consistently trades force for mobility — one reason joints and tendons, not muscles,
    are so often the failure points.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Torque</strong> — force times perpendicular distance from the joint axis — is the
    currency of all joint function, and it changes continuously with position. The plot below
    makes the point concrete: hold a 5 kg weight with the forearm horizontal (0.3 m forearm) and
    the external torque at the elbow is 5 × 9.81 × 0.30 ≈ 14.7 N·m; rotate the forearm toward
    vertical and the same load becomes almost effortless, because the load's moment arm shrinks
    with the cosine of the angle. Every clinical fact about "harder at this angle, easier at that
    one" is this cosine. Meanwhile muscle has its own geometry-dependent behaviour: the
    <strong>force–length relationship</strong> (tension peaks near mid-range, where actin–myosin
    filament overlap is optimal, and falls at extremes) and the <strong>force–velocity
    relationship</strong> (Hill's curve: a muscle shortening fast produces much less force than
    one contracting slowly — and even less eccentrically loaded control fails at high speed).
    Internal torque capability is therefore itself an angle-dependent curve, and the patient's
    functional failure occurs wherever the external demand curve comes closest to the internal
    capability curve. Isokinetic dynamometry, manual muscle testing positions, and the "sticking
    point" of any lift all exist because strength is not one number but a curve — and therapy
    that trains only one angle of it leaves the rest untrained.
  </p>
  <p>
    Biomechanics extends beyond the joint into <strong>tissue mechanics</strong>: tendons,
    ligaments, cartilage and bone are viscoelastic materials whose response depends on load rate.
    Sustained stretch produces <em>creep</em> (progressive lengthening under constant load) and
    repeated loading below tolerance stimulates adaptation — Wolff's law for bone (it remodels
    along the lines of stress) and the equivalent for tendon and muscle, the basis of graded
    loading in tendinopathy rehabilitation. Push the same tissues too fast and failure wins:
    ligaments sprain at rates they would have tolerated slowly. Therapy lives on this dose–response
    curve, between too little load (no adaptation) and too much (injury) — the window the
    profession calls therapeutic loading.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="external torque of a held load rises and falls with the cosine of joint angle — drag the sliders" widget="plotter" config={elbowCfg} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that "lactic acid" causes next-day muscle soreness. The lactate produced in hard exercise is cleared within the hour and is in fact a fuel and signalling molecule; delayed-onset muscle soreness (DOMS) is micro-damage and inflammation from unaccustomed eccentric loading. The mistake matters clinically, because patients who believe soreness means "acid buildup" stretch, rest excessively or avoid the very progressive loading that would adapt their muscles.</p>

  <h2>Exercise physiology</h2>
  <p>
    Exercise physiology treats the body as an energy system that adapts to demand, and its core
    model is <strong>three energy systems operating in series</strong>. The phosphagen system
    (stored ATP plus phosphocreatine) fuels about ten seconds of truly maximal effort — the sprint
    start, the single heavy lift. Anaerobic glycolysis bridges the next tens of seconds to a
    couple of minutes, regenerating ATP rapidly but at the cost of accumulating metabolites — the
    burn of a 400 m race. Oxidative metabolism carries everything beyond, limited by oxygen
    delivery (lungs, heart, blood) and utilisation (mitochondrial density, capillarisation) —
    which is why the marathon is an oxygen-economy event and the weightlift is not. Training is
    largely the art of choosing which system to stress: heavy resistance for neural and
    hypertrophic adaptation, intervals at high intensity for the glycolytic ceiling, long
    moderate work for the oxidative base.
  </p>
  <p>
    The aerobic ceiling is <strong>VO<sub>2</sub>max</strong>: maximal oxygen uptake in
    ml/kg/min, the single best index of cardiorespiratory fitness. An untrained adult sits near
    30–40; an elite endurance athlete near 70–90. Training raises it typically 15–25 per cent
    through central adaptations (a bigger stroke volume — the heart's per-beat output — is the
    headline) and peripheral ones (more capillaries and mitochondria in muscle). Just below the
    ceiling lies the <strong>lactate threshold</strong>, the intensity at which lactate
    accumulates faster than it can be cleared — the better predictor of endurance performance,
    and highly trainable even when VO<sub>2</sub>max is not. Prescription turns this physiology
    into a dose, classically summarised as <strong>FITT-VP</strong>: Frequency, Intensity, Time,
    Type, Volume and Progression. Intensity becomes a number via the Karvonen formula above,
    which scales target heart rate to the heart-rate reserve. Worked example: a 50-year-old with
    resting heart rate 70 (Tanaka HR<sub>max</sub> = 208 − 0.7 × 50 = 173) training at 60 per
    cent of reserve gets 70 + 0.6 × (173 − 70) ≈ 132 beats per minute — a number the patient can
    watch on any pulse monitor, which is what makes physiology clinical rather than academic.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two further principles govern everything a physiotherapist prescribes. <strong>Specificity</strong>:
    adaptations are specific to the muscles, speeds, angles and energy systems trained — cycling
    fitness transfers imperfectly to running; strength at one joint angle transfers weakly to
    others (see the torque curves above). <strong>Reversibility</strong>: adaptation is rented,
    not owned, and deconditioning obeys the dose–response law in reverse. The classic Dallas
    bed-rest studies found young subjects losing roughly a quarter to a third of their aerobic
    capacity in three weeks of strict bed rest — which is why early mobilisation after surgery or
    stroke is a medical prescription with doses and targets, not a courtesy, and why "just keep
    moving" is the most evidence-based sentence in the profession.
  </p>

  <h2>Motor control and learning</h2>
  <p>
    Strength and fitness are useless if the nervous system cannot deploy them — and after stroke,
    injury or simply a new prosthesis, the nervous system is often the missing piece. Motor
    control studies how movements are organised: the synergy of muscles recruited, the role of
    sensory feedback (vision, vestibular input, proprioception) in postural control and balance,
    and how control changes with pathology. Motor learning studies how practice changes it. Fitts
    and Posner's three stages — <strong>cognitive</strong> (performing by thinking, effortful,
    error-ridden), <strong>associative</strong> (refining, fewer gross errors), <strong>autonomous</strong>
    (performed while attention is elsewhere) — describe every patient's journey from thinking
    through a sit-to-stand to standing up while holding a conversation, and each stage wants a
    different clinic: instructions and demonstration early, variable challenge in the middle,
    real-world dual-task practice at the end.
  </p>
  <p>
    Practice design is where the science earns its keep, and its findings are famously
    counter-intuitive. Schmidt's <strong>schema theory</strong> holds that the motor program is
    tuned by comparing predicted with actual sensory consequences, so the system learns most from
    <em>variability</em> and error — not from flawless repetition. Hence <strong>blocked practice</strong>
    (AAA-BBB-CCC) produces beautiful sessions but poor retention, while <strong>random practice</strong>
    (ABC-BCA-CAB) feels worse and <em>retains</em> better — the contextual-interference effect,
    confirmed across skills from rehabilitation to surgery. Feedback follows the same logic:
    every-trial extrinsic knowledge of results creates dependency; faded, bandwidth or delayed
    feedback forces the patient to develop their own intrinsic error detection — the capacity
    they will need at home when the therapist is not there. The end goal of therapy is therefore
    not a better Wednesday session; it is a movement the patient owns, in their own kitchen,
    while tired and distracted.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "practice makes perfect." Practice makes <em>permanent</em> — of whatever was practised. Ten thousand flawless repetitions of a compensatory pattern (the hitched shoulder, the locked knee) build a permanent compensation that is harder to unlearn than the original skill would have been to acquire. Quality and variability of practice outrank quantity, which is the quiet revolution of motor-learning research in every rehabilitation gym.</p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three deeper threads connect the sciences to modern practice. First, <strong>spine biomechanics
    as a case study in cumulative load</strong>: a 10 kg load held 50 cm in front of the
    L5/S1 disc produces about 49 N·m of trunk torque before the trunk's own weight is counted,
    and the erector spinae — short moment arms again — multiply disc compression several-fold;
    holding the load close to the body is not etiquette, it is lever arithmetic, and it is why
    manual-handling training is biomechanics in overalls. Second, <strong>power and rate of force
    development</strong> increasingly displace raw strength in geriatric programmes: rising from
    a chair or recovering from a trip takes under half a second, so what fails in falling is not
    maximum torque but the speed of reaching it — training that is heavy, fast-safe and
    task-specific (sit-to-stand repetitions, step-ups) outperforms slow machine work for exactly
    the outcome that matters. Third, <strong>neuroplasticity constraints</strong> give motor
    learning its biological frame: cortical remapping responds to intensity, repetition,
    salience and specificity of use, which is the neuroscience behind constraint-induced movement
    therapy after stroke — forcing use of the affected limb reshapes the map, while
    compensation leaves it idle. The three sciences meet in every treatment plan: biomechanics
    decides what load, physiology decides what dose, motor learning decides what practice — and
    the art of physiotherapy is the arithmetic that makes all three fit one patient, one
    session, one goal at a time.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Norkin &amp; Levangie, Joint Structure and Function; McArdle, Katch &amp; Katch, Exercise Physiology; Shumway-Cook &amp; Woollacott, Motor Control: Translating Research into Clinical Practice.</p>
</TopicPage>
